Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||07-30-2001||04:04 PM||||you cannot replace the bushings in this particular dog bone because their is a cast-in rib dividing the two halves, making an "hour-glass" shape in the bushing area - you simply will have to replace the dog bone||||reg||1
Z||000004||Capt Fiero||07-30-2001||04:58 PM||||I do have a local shop that does the maching of the dog bones for me, the only catch is that the later dog-bones are about a 1/4 - 1/2 inch longer.  Where are you located.  I retail poly dog-bones for 30$ CDN about 20$ US funds.  They are not the fancy ones that you get from the Fiero store. They are just a used 85-86 V6 style dogbone with poly inserts, they hold up well and have a free replacment if broken or cracked in  2 years.  I am in BC Canada near Vancouver.   Sorry I dont have a web page for the dog bones as up until now I do them for our car club only.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||07-30-2001||05:53 PM||||I ended up having to replace the dogbone on my 88 coupe in Florida last fall. Finally found a parts store that said they could have one the next day. When I got there it was the short V6 style. Sent it back, got another one next day... same thing.. <P>They told me that I was wrong and I must be mistaken. Their books showed this was right! Well if the book says so..... duh! I took them out into the parking lot and showed then their book was not right..<P>Finally decided to take a chance at a Pontiac dealership. They ordered me a new GM dogbone, had it in the next day and cost me less than what the parts store was trying to sell me... I believe I paid $36 for it..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
